## The theme is a single source of truth

Colours and figure geometry come from `conf/theme/`. The library validates them
(every role present in both light and dark) and renders a Matplotlib style:

```{code-cell} ipython3
from {{ package_name }} import theming

base = {
    "roles": ["background", "foreground", "accent"],
    "fonts": {"family": "DejaVu Sans", "base_pt": 8,
              "tick_pt": 7, "legend_pt": 7},
    "figure": {"width_in": 3.5, "height_in": 2.5, "dpi": 300, "spine_pt": 0.6,
               "tick_len_pt": 3.5, "tick_width_pt": 0.5, "line_pt": 1.2,
               "marker_pt": 4.5},
}
light = {"background": "#FFFFFF", "foreground": "#1A1A1A", "accent": "#0072B2"}
dark = {"background": "#00000000", "foreground": "#EAEAEA", "accent": "#56B4E9"}

theme = theming.load_theme(base, light, dark)
print(theming.render_mplstyle(theme, "light").splitlines()[0])
```

## Load data by name (the catalog)

Datasets are named once in `conf/catalog.yaml`, so code loads a logical key
instead of hard-coding paths and formats. Here is the API on a self-contained
example:

```{code-cell} ipython3
import tempfile
import os
from pathlib import Path

from {{ package_name }} import catalog

tmp = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp())
(tmp / "s.csv").write_text("id,signal\n1,0.5\n2,0.7\n")
cat = catalog.Catalog(datasets={
    "samples": {"path": "s.csv", "format": "csv"},
    "provenance": {"path": "results/provenance.json", "format": "json"},
})
original = Path.cwd()
try:
    os.chdir(tmp)
    print("samples ->", catalog.dataset_path("samples", cat))
    frame = catalog.load_dataset("samples", cat)
finally:
    os.chdir(original)
frame
```

## A themed figure

```{code-cell} ipython3
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

with plt.style.context({"axes.prop_cycle": plt.cycler(color=["#0072B2"])}):
    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(3.5, 2.5))
    ax.plot(rng.normal(size=50).cumsum())
    ax.set_xlabel("step")
    ax.set_ylabel("value")
fig
```
